<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Duowen Chen
</span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Yan Wang<span id="footnotex1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotemark"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotemark: </span>
          <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
          
          
          
          
          
          
          
        </span></span></span>
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Shanghai Key Laboratory of Multidimensional Information Processing, East China Normal University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><span id="id1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">duowen<span id="id1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline"> </span>chen@hotmail.com, ywang@cee.ecnu.edu.cn</span>
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span><a href="https://github.com/DuowenC/FSSLlib" title="" class="ltx_ref ltx_url ltx_font_typewriter" style="font-size:90%;">https://github.com/DuowenC/FSSLlib</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Federated Semi-Supervised Learning (FSSL) aims to collaboratively train a global model across clients by leveraging partially-annotated local data in a privacy-preserving manner. In FSSL, data heterogeneity is a challenging issue, which exists both across clients and within clients. External heterogeneity refers to the data distribution discrepancy across different clients, while internal heterogeneity represents the mismatch between labeled and unlabeled data within clients.
Most FSSL methods typically design fixed or dynamic parameter aggregation strategies to collect client knowledge on the server (external) and / or filter out low-confidence unlabeled samples to reduce mistakes in local client (internal). But, the former is hard to precisely fit the ideal global distribution via direct weights, and the latter results in fewer data participation into FL training. To this end, we propose a proxy-guided framework called ProxyFL that focuses on simultaneously mitigating external and internal heterogeneity via a unified proxy. I.e., we consider the learnable weights of classifier as proxy to simulate the category distribution both locally and globally. For external, we explicitly optimize global proxy against outliers instead of direct weights; for internal, we re-include the discarded samples into training by a positive-negative proxy pool to mitigate the impact of potentially-incorrect pseudo-labels. Insight experiments &amp; theoretical analysis show our significant performance and convergence in FSSL.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="p1.1.1" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div><span id="footnotex2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnotetext"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">*</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">footnotetext: </span>Corresponding Author.</span></span></span>
<figure id="S0.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21078v1/draft-fig111108.png" id="S0.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:685/655;" width="685" height="655" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S0.F1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S0.F1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
(a) Illustration of centralized learning and averaging-based federated learning. (b) Differences of test accuracy and pseudo-labeling accuracy under varying levels of heterogeneity (smaller <math id="S0.F1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> indicates greater heterogeneity). During each communication round, all clients are trained based on FedSGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> <em id="S0.F1.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/</em>, <em id="S0.F1.6.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o</em> low-confidence samples and our method for one local epoch.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The rapid advancement of edge devices and the Internet of Things (IoT) has led to a pressing need for decentralized training paradigms <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>. Federated learning (FL), a distributed machine-learning paradigm, facilitates multi-device collaborative learning without sacrificing data privacy, which shares only model updates rather than raw data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite>. Most existing FL works assume that local data in clients are fully labeled, but this assumption does not hold in practical scenarios when data annotation is laborious, time-consuming, or expensive. To remedy these issues, Federated Semi-Supervised Learning (FSSL) has emerged, enabling clients to train models leveraging both limited labeled data and a large amount of unlabeled data, thereby improving the performance of global model. In FSSL, data heterogeneity exists both across clients (external heterogeneity) and within clients (internal heterogeneity). The former refers to the distribution discrepancy across different clients, while the latter arises from the local mismatch due to 1) imbalanced sample sizes across different categories; 2) distribution imbalance between labeled &amp; unlabeled data.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">external heterogeneity</span>, most studies design the dynamic aggregation weights to mitigate the imbalance across clients, based on relative sizes of local datasets <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> or some implicit statistics of local samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite>, which may deviate from the ideal global distribution; For <span id="S1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">internal heterogeneity</span>, some methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite> typically filter high-confidence unlabeled samples for training and exclude low-confidence ones to avoid pseudo-label bias, or dynamically assign a smaller weight to low-confidence samples, <em id="S1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, FedDure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>. However, these methods either lead to fewer data participation due to discarding low-confidence data or compromise on incorrect pseudo-labels only via a smaller weight. These insights motivate us to pose the following questions: ① <em id="S1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Is there a better way than the aggregation weights to fit the global distribution without privacy concerns?</em> ② <em id="S1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">How can the local model leverage low-confidence unlabeled samples more effectively?</em></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">To this end, we conduct some investigations into the above questions. As illustrated in Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(a), the averaging-based aggregation strategy may lead to model deviation from the global category space, due to the distribution heterogeneity across clients; Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>(b) show test accuracy &amp; pseudo-labeling accuracy under different levels of heterogeneity. We observe that, as heterogeneity varies, training <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/</em> and <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o</em> low confidence samples exhibit opposite trends to model performance. <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">I.e.</em>, with greater heterogeneity (<em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>), the model ability is very limited, so <em id="S1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/ low-conf</em> outperforms <em id="S1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o low-conf</em> due to the inclusion of more training samples. But, when the labeling ability of the model is more reliable under lower heterogeneity (<em id="S1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>), incorporating extra low-confidence samples (<em id="S1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/ low-conf</em>) may hinder the model’s category learning due to the errors from low-confidence pseudo-labels. In short, the performance of the two methods (<em id="S1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/</em> and <em id="S1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o low-conf</em>) is inconsistent across different levels of heterogeneity. Neither method shows clear superiority over the other. So, it is crucial to consider how to utilize low-confidence samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">To tackle Question ① and ② of the above observations in FSSL, we propose a new method called <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ProxyFL</span> (<span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proxy</span>-Guided <span id="S1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">F</span>ederated Semi-Supervised <span id="S1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>earning) that <span id="S1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">leverages a unified proxy to simultaneously mitigate both internal and external heterogeneity</span>. <em id="S1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">I.e.</em>, we consider the learnable weights of classifier as proxies to model the category distribution both locally and globally. <span id="S1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proxy does not sacrifice data privacy and bring negligible extra communication costs since the proxy itself is part of the model parameters in FL.</span> Firstly, we introduce a <span id="S1.p4.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">G</span>lobal <span id="S1.p4.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>roxy-<span id="S1.p4.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">T</span>uning (GPT) mechanism, which explicitly optimize global proxies to fit the category distribution across clients, mitigating distribution shift from external heterogeneity. Secondly, we incorporate low-confidence unlabeled samples via a dynamic <span id="S1.p4.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>ndecisive-<span id="S1.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C</span>ategories <span id="S1.p4.1.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">P</span>roxy <span id="S1.p4.1.14" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">L</span>earning (ICPL) mechanism. For each low-confidence sample, we propose an <em id="S1.p4.1.15" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">indecisive-categories set</em> to represent its several possible categories instead of a single pseudo-label; For high-confidence unlabeled samples or labeled samples, we utilize the <em id="S1.p4.1.16" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pseudo-label</em> or <em id="S1.p4.1.17" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ground-truth</em>, respectively. Then we propose a relationship pool between unlabeled and labeled samples, and effectively train all samples based on the pool to mitigate internal heterogeneity. Experiments show that ProxyFL can significantly boost the performance and convergence of the FSSL model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this paper are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To our best knowledge, this paper is the first to propose a unified proxy to mitigate both internal and external heterogeneity in FSSL. Note that our proxy does not bring privacy concerns or extra communication costs.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper proposes an FSSL method, ProxyFL, that can not only reduce the bias of averaging-based aggregation weights via an explicit optimization objective, but also effectively build the category relationship among all samples to facilitate more data participation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper outperforms existing FSSL methods across multiple datasets. Extensive empirical &amp; theoretical analysis also show the effectiveness and convergence of our method under different levels of heterogeneity.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Federated Learning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Federated learning (FL) is a distributed machine learning approach that focus on safeguarding data privacy. One of the most challenging issues in FL is data heterogeneity, <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, the distributions across clients are <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">non</span>-<span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">i</span>ndependent and <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">i</span>dentically <span id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">d</span>istributed (non-i.i.d). To this end, various FL methods have been proposed to address this challenge such as regularization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, additional data sharing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, aggregation strategies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, and personalization <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. For instance, FedProx <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> requires each client to regularize with the previous global parameters to reduce local bias. However, these fully-supervised FL approaches struggle to generalize well under the scenarios of annotation scarcity. To address this issue, Federated Semi-Supervised Learning (FSSL) emerges, which enables clients to train local models via limited labeled data &amp; unlabeled data.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Semi-Supervised Learning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Semi-Supervised Learning (SSL) aims to effectively leverage both limited labeled data and a large amount of unlabeled data to improve model performance. Two commonly-used strategies in SSL are consistency regularization and pseudo-labeling, respectively. Consistency regularization is based on the assumption that a model’s prediction should remain consistent despite diverse perturbations to the inputs or model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite>. Another common strategy is pseudo-labeling, which determines pseudo-labels for unlabeled samples based on the high-confidence predictions of from the pre-trained model by labeled data and filter out low-confidence unlabeled samples <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>]</cite>. However, the pseudo-labels of these methods heavily depend on the confidence scores of model predictions, and if simply transferring them to the FL field, the number of local data will further decline due to the exclusion of low-confidence unlabeled samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Federated Semi-Supervised Learning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Federated Semi-Supervised Learning (FSSL) addresses the challenge of training models on decentralized data where annotations are scarce. The field is often categorized into three scenarios: 1) Labels-at-Server, where clients only have unlabeled data and a central server holds labeled data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">8</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>. 2) Label-at-All-Client, where every client has a small fraction of labeled data and a large amount of unlabeled data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite>. 3) Labels-at-Partial-Clients, where a few clients are fully labeled while others are unlabeled <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>]</cite>. Our work focuses on the Label-at-All-Client setting, where a small fraction of samples have the ground-truths labels in each client, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Problem Formulation</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This paper focuses on data heterogeneity in FSSL, including not only <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">external heterogeneity<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">†</span></span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            <span id="footnote2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">Definitions of FSSL data heterogeneity in Appendix </span><a href="#A1" title="Appendix A Definitions of Data Heterogeneity in FSSL ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_medium"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a><span id="footnote2.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">. </span></span></span></span></span> across clients but also <span id="S3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">internal heterogeneity<sup id="S3.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_sup"><a href="#footnote2" title="Footnote † ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref ltx_font_medium"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">†</span> ‣ <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a></sup></span> between labeled and unlabeled distributions within each client. Specifically, we assume that a federation system <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℂ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> consists of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> clients, denoted as <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}=\{\mathcal{C}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{C}_{K}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℂ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>K</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}=\{\mathcal{C}_{1},\ldots,\mathcal{C}_{K}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. For each <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, its local model is parameterized by <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Theta}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Theta}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, which comprises a feature extractor <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, projecting local data to an embedding space <math id="S3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a classifier <math id="S3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="h_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> parameterized by <math id="S3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, mapping the embedding space to category space <math id="S3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> indicates total category number. <em id="S3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">I.e.</em>, <math id="S3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Theta}_{k}=\theta_{k}\cup\omega_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∪</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Theta}_{k}=\theta_{k}\cup\omega_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21078v1/fig2new0109.png" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:617/661;" width="617" height="661" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
(a) t-SNE visualization of <span id="S3.F2.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">classifier weights</span> from clients (circle) during the initial round. Different colors means different categories. Pentagram denotes the simple average of weight parameters by category. (b) <span id="S3.F2.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Number of excluded unlabeled samples</span> under different levels of heterogeneity. (c) <span id="S3.F2.9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Test accuracy curves</span> of ‘SGD-FSSL’ <em id="S3.F2.9.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/o</em> and <em id="S3.F2.9.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">w/</em> low-confidence samples under different ratios of correct labels. These samples could improve performance as the correctly-labeled number increases.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each client <math id="S3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> maintains a private partially-labeled dataset <math id="S3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>, including labeled samples <math id="S3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{k}^{\texttt{{s}}}{=}\{\mathbf{x}_{k,i},\mathbf{y}_{k,i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{k}^{\texttt{{s}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">s</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">s</mtext></msubsup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{k}^{\texttt{{s}}}{=}\{\mathbf{x}_{k,i},\mathbf{y}_{k,i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{k}^{\texttt{{s}}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and unlabeled samples <math id="S3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{k}^{\texttt{{u}}}{=}\{\mathbf{u}_{k,i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{k}^{\texttt{{u}}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">u</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">u</mtext></msubsup></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{k}^{\texttt{{u}}}{=}\{\mathbf{u}_{k,i}\}_{i=1}^{N_{k}^{\texttt{{u}}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{k}^{\texttt{{s}}}\ll N_{k}^{\texttt{{u}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">s</mtext></msubsup><mo>≪</mo><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>k</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">u</mtext></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{k}^{\texttt{{s}}}\ll N_{k}^{\texttt{{u}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. During each communication round, a subset of online clients <math id="S3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{C}_{M}\subseteq\mathbb{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mi>ℂ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{C}_{M}\subseteq\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math> is randomly selected for local training <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>. Then, FSSL methods typically aggregate the uploaded local parameters <math id="S3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathbf{\Theta}_{m}\}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathbf{\Theta}_{m}\}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}}</annotation></semantics></math> as the global parameters <math id="S3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Theta}_{\mathcal{G}}=\sum_{\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}}\gamma_{m}\mathbf{\Theta}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Theta}_{\mathcal{G}}=\sum_{\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}}\gamma_{m}\mathbf{\Theta}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, where the aggregation weight <math id="S3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> is empirically set by the proportion of its local dataset size relative to the total samples across <math id="S3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> clients.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Preliminary Study</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this study, we conduct some exploratory experiments for Question ① and ② in Sec. <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21078v1/framework-cvpr11151.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:617/203;" width="617" height="203" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of <span id="S4.F3.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ProxyFL</span> for Global Proxy Tuning in server-side &amp; Indecisive-Categories Proxy Learning in local client.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>, the weight parameters of the network classifier have a certain ability in category representation. Thus, we extract the classifier weights <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\omega_{m}\}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}},~{\omega_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times d}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\omega_{m}\}_{\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}},~{\omega_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{C\times d}}</annotation></semantics></math> from clients and slice them by class to generate a t-SNE plot, thereby visually showing the external heterogeneity to discuss Question ①.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(a), directly using centroids (<em id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, pentagrams, the averaging-based weights) may not accurately fit the global category distribution across clients. Due to external heterogeneity, some clients exhibit significant distribution discrepancy from others, <span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">causing some points to be outliers</span>. So, the averaging-based method is affected by these outliers, positioning the centroid outside most points of the category cluster. We summarize as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmobservation1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmobservation1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmobservation1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmobservation1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmobservation1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Simply averaging classifier weights is prone to skew towards outliers, thus failing to effectively capture the global category distribution across clients.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For <span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">internal heterogeneity</span>, most FSSL methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> follow FixMatch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> to keep high-confident unlabeled samples while ignoring low-confidence ones. This allows the model to heavily rely on limited easy-to-judge unlabeled samples and exacerbates internal heterogeneity. We examine internal heterogeneity in Fig. <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3 Problem Formulation ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>(b-c) and observe that:</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmobservation2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_observation">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmobservation2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Observation 2</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmobservation2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmobservation2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmobservation2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">As data heterogeneity increases, more unlabeled samples will be excluded from local training; The excluded samples have the potential to boost performance.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the above observations, we propose our <em id="S4.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ProxyFL</em> from two perspectives, <em id="S4.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, <span id="S4.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">how to fit a global category distribution robustly against outliers (external) and how to effectively leverage low-confidence unlabeled samples (internal)</span>. To this end, we propose to leverage <span id="S4.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the learnable weights of model classifier as proxy</span> for jointly modeling local &amp; global category distribution of FSSL, instead of prototypes (See Sec. <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Empirical Analysis ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>). Specifically, for client <math id="S4.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, we define its proxy vectors <math id="S4.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{\omega}_{m}^{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{\omega}_{m}^{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S4.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, the final FC layer of model classifier, to represent each category, where <math id="S4.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega^{c}_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>d</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega^{c}_{m}\in\mathbb{R}^{d}</annotation></semantics></math>. Our framework is shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Preliminary Study ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Global Proxy Tuning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To mitigate the impact of external heterogeneity (Obs. <a href="#Thmobservation1" title="Observation 1 ‣ 4 Preliminary Study ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), we propose to model the category distribution across clients <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}(\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}(\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> on the central server via learning a set of global proxies <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}=\{\mathcal{\omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}=\{\mathcal{\omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, called <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">G</span>lobal <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">P</span>roxy <span id="S5.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">T</span>uning (GPT). In each federation round, the server receives model parameters from each client, thus a straight-forward idea to aggregate local classifiers is <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathcal{G}}{=}\{\sum_{\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}}\gamma_{m}\mathbf{\omega}_{m}^{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛀</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><msub><mo lspace="0em">∑</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><msub><mi>γ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathcal{G}}{=}\{\sum_{\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}}\gamma_{m}\mathbf{\omega}_{m}^{c}\}_{c=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S5.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛀</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> means the average operation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">However, as summarized in Obs. <a href="#Thmobservation1" title="Observation 1 ‣ 4 Preliminary Study ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, simply averaging the local proxies is prone to be affected by the outliers due to external heterogeneity. Therefore, we first initialize the global proxies <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝛀</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{\Omega}}_{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> and then further fine-tune <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> on the server by leveraging the off-the-shelf uploaded local proxies <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mathcal{\omega}_{m}^{c}\}_{c=1}^{C},~\forall~\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">∀</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℂ</mi><mi>M</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mathcal{\omega}_{m}^{c}\}_{c=1}^{C},~\forall~\mathcal{C}_{m}\in\mathbb{C}_{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. More concretely, for the global proxy <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c}}</annotation></semantics></math> of category <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math>, our objective is to pull it closer to all local proxies belonging to category <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>c</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c</annotation></semantics></math> and push it away from local proxies of other categories. The optimization objective of <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}}\sum_{c=1}^{C}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\left[\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},\omega_{m}^{c})-\sum_{c^{\prime}=1,c^{\prime}\neq c}^{C}\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},\omega_{m}^{c^{\prime}})\right]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub></munder><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover></mstyle><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo>≠</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></munderover></mstyle><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>c</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\min_{\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}}\sum_{c=1}^{C}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\left[\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},\omega_{m}^{c})-\sum_{c^{\prime}=1,c^{\prime}\neq c}^{C}\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},\omega_{m}^{c^{\prime}})\right],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(\cdot,\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo rspace="0em">,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(\cdot,\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> refers to the distance metric. Given the above objective, we formulate the loss function for <span id="S5.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">G</span>lobal <span id="S5.SS1.p2.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">P</span>roxy <span id="S5.SS1.p2.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">T</span>uning (GPT) as follows:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\texttt{{GPT}}}=\sum_{c=1}^{C}\sum_{m=1}^{M}-log\frac{e^{-\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},~\omega_{m}^{c})}}{e^{-\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},~\omega_{m}^{c})}+\sum_{c^{\prime}=1,c^{\prime}\neq c}^{C}e^{-\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},~\omega_{m}^{c^{\prime}})}}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">ℒ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathsize="0.800em">GPT</mtext></msub><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">C</mi></msup><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo></mstyle><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">M</mi></msup><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">l</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">o</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">g</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒢</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">ω</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒢</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">ω</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em" rspace="0.055em">+</mo><mrow><msubsup><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.800em">1</mn></mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><mrow><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">′</mo></msup><mo mathsize="0.800em">≠</mo><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi></mrow></mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">C</mi></msubsup><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">e</mi><mrow><mo mathsize="0.800em">−</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.800em">ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">(</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.800em">𝒢</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi></msubsup><mo mathsize="0.800em">,</mo><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.800em">ω</mi><mi mathsize="0.800em">m</mi><msup><mi mathsize="0.800em">c</mi><mo mathsize="0.800em">′</mo></msup></msubsup><mo maxsize="0.800em" minsize="0.800em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" mathsize="0.800em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\texttt{{GPT}}}=\sum_{c=1}^{C}\sum_{m=1}^{M}-log\frac{e^{-\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},~\omega_{m}^{c})}}{e^{-\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},~\omega_{m}^{c})}+\sum_{c^{\prime}=1,c^{\prime}\neq c}^{C}e^{-\phi(\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}^{c},~\omega_{m}^{c^{\prime}})}}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The entire tuning process of GPT module is conducted on the server. Then, the well-tuned <math id="S5.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> are sent to each client together with other aggregated parameters, as the parametric initialization for the next round of local training.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.2 </span>Indecisive-Categories Proxy Learning</h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As noted in Obs. <a href="#Thmobservation2" title="Observation 2 ‣ 4 Preliminary Study ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, higher internal heterogeneity leads to fewer data participation. To this end, we propose <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">I</span>ndecisive-<span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">C</span>ategories <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">P</span>roxy <span id="S5.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_underline">L</span>earning (ICPL) to incorporate more low-confidence unlabeled samples.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For an unlabeled sample <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">‡</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">‡</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">‡</span>
              
              
              
              
              
              
              
            Note that in this section, we drop the client subscript <math id="footnote3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> in data samples for symbolic simplicity.</span></span></span>,
its <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">local feature</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">local logits</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> and <span id="S5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">global logits</span> <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{\Theta}_{\mathcal{G}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝚯</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{\Theta}_{\mathcal{G}}</annotation></semantics></math> can be calculated as:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle z_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle z_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=f_{m}\left(\mathcal{T}_{w}(\mathbf{u}_{i});\theta_{m}\right),\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{i}=h_{m}\left(z_{i};\omega_{m}\right),\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>ω</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=f_{m}\left(\mathcal{T}_{w}(\mathbf{u}_{i});\theta_{m}\right),\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{i}=h_{m}\left(z_{i};\omega_{m}\right),\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}=h_{\mathcal{G}}((f_{\mathcal{G}}(\mathcal{T}_{w}(\mathbf{u}_{i});\theta_{\mathcal{G}}));\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}),\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>h</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>𝛀</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mi>C</mi></msup></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}=h_{\mathcal{G}}((f_{\mathcal{G}}(\mathcal{T}_{w}(\mathbf{u}_{i});\theta_{\mathcal{G}}));\mathbf{\Omega}_{\mathcal{G}}),\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}\in\mathbb{R}^{C},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{T}_{w}(\cdot)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒯</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{T}_{w}(\cdot)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the weak augmentation.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.1 </span>Category Set Construction</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We first construct its category set for each sample in local client as follows: <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I. Labeled Samples.</span> As shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Preliminary Study ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> upper right, for a labeled sample <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, its category set is defined by its ground-truth as <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}=\{\mathbf{y}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}=\{\mathbf{y}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a single-element set; <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">II. High-Confidence Unlabeled Samples.</span> In Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Preliminary Study ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> middle right, if <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i})&gt;\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i})&gt;\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is a high-confidence sample (denoted as <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{hc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">hc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{hc}}}</annotation></semantics></math>) with its pseudo-label <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{i}=\arg\max(\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{i}=\arg\max(\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. Here, we define its <em id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">category set</em> <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}=\{\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}=\{\hat{\mathbf{y}}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as a single-element set like <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>;
<span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">III. Low-Confidence Unlabeled Samples.</span> In Fig. <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4 Preliminary Study ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> lower right, if <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max(\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i})\leq\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>τ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max(\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i})\leq\tau</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is regarded as a low-confidence sample, denoted as <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, a simple low-confident pseudo-label may affect model performance due to the potential labeling errors. Prior study <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>]</cite> shows the effectiveness of assigning more than one category labels to ambiguous ROI candidates. Thus, we leverage <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the several categories among which the model hesitates</span> to represent its possible labels for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, defined as <em id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">indecisive-categories set</em> <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\xi}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\xi}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, <em id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g., <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">{</span>mouse, hamster<span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">}</span></em> in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.2.1 Category Set Construction ‣ 5.2 Indecisive-Categories Proxy Learning ‣ 5 Methodology ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(a-b). To better determine <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ξ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi</annotation></semantics></math>, we dynamically maintain a global category prior <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> to constrain the <em id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">indecisive categories</em> for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S5.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\xi_{i}=\{c|\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}(c)&gt;\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y}(c))\}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\xi_{i}=\{c|\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}(c)&gt;\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y}(c))\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in[1,C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in[1,C]</annotation></semantics></math>. So for <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}^{\texttt{{lc}}}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}^{\texttt{{lc}}}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>, any category <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in[1,C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in[1,C]</annotation></semantics></math> with corresponding global logits <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathbf{y}}_{i}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> exceeding <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y}(c))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y}(c))</annotation></semantics></math> is considered as <em id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">indecisive category</em>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Prior Distribution <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> In each communication round <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, we locally calculate model preference <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{m}}(\mathbf{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi>m</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{m}}(\mathbf{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> for category prediction, without the need of real per-class number for data privacy.
Then, they are uploaded to the server for aggregation, denoted as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S5.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{m}}(\mathbf{Y})=\{\frac{\overline{N^{\texttt{{s,hc}}}_{m}}(c)}{\overline{N^{\texttt{{s,hc}}}_{m}}}\}_{c=1}^{C},\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{\mathcal{G}}}(\mathbf{Y})=\{\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{m}}(\mathbf{Y}(c))\}_{c=1}^{C}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒫</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐘</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathsize="0.700em">s,hc</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">c</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi mathsize="0.700em">N</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace" mathsize="0.700em">s,hc</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">c</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">C</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒢</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐘</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munderover><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" movablelimits="false" stretchy="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">M</mi></munderover></mstyle><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.700em">𝒫</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">m</mi><mi mathsize="0.700em">t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">𝐘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">(</mo><mi mathsize="0.700em">c</mi><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">c</mi><mo mathsize="0.700em">=</mo><mn mathsize="0.700em">1</mn></mrow><mi mathsize="0.700em">C</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.700em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{m}}(\mathbf{Y})=\{\frac{\overline{N^{\texttt{{s,hc}}}_{m}}(c)}{\overline{N^{\texttt{{s,hc}}}_{m}}}\}_{c=1}^{C},\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{\mathcal{G}}}(\mathbf{Y})=\{\frac{1}{M}\sum_{m=1}^{M}\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{m}}(\mathbf{Y}(c))\}_{c=1}^{C},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y})\leftarrow\{\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{\mathcal{G}}}(\mathbf{Y}(c))\}_{c=1}^{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>′</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝐘</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mi>t</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>𝐘</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>C</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{P}_{\mathcal{G}}^{\prime}(\mathbf{Y})\leftarrow\{\overline{\mathcal{P}^{t}_{\mathcal{G}}}(\mathbf{Y}(c))\}_{c=1}^{C}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\overline{N^{\texttt{{s,hc}}}_{m}}(c)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><msubsup><mi>N</mi><mi>m</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">s,hc</mtext></msubsup><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\overline{N^{\texttt{{s,hc}}}_{m}}(c)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the prediction counts of each category from labeled and high-confidence samples.
In ICPL, it acts as a dynamic threshold for different classes by setting a higher threshold for the majority classes and lower for the minority.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F4" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21078v1/mpartnew0113.png" id="S5.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:685/416;" width="685" height="416" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S5.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a-b) Examples of the indecisive-categories. (c) Illustration of our Indecisive-Categories Proxy Learning.

</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">5.2.2 </span>Positive-Negative Proxy Pool</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">category set</span> of each sample, we build <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the relationship among all types of samples</span> and train them by Contrastive Learning (CL) to mitigate internal heterogeneity.
To this end, we construct a ‘Positive-Negative Proxy Pool’ as follows (shown in Fig. <a href="#S5.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 5.2.1 Category Set Construction ‣ 5.2 Indecisive-Categories Proxy Learning ‣ 5 Methodology ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>(c)):</p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(Positive-Negative Proxy Pool)</span><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For a batch <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{m}</annotation></semantics></math>, we construct the Positive-Negative Proxy Pool without excluding any of the samples. </span><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1) Positive Proxy.</span><span id="Thmremark1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> The positive proxy of a high-confidence sample <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{hc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">hc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{hc}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and a low-confidence sample <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}</annotation></semantics></math> are respectively defined as:</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\omega_{i}^{\texttt{{hc}}}=\omega_{k}^{\hat{\mathbf{y}_{i}}},~\omega_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}=\sum_{\forall{c’}\in\xi_{i}}\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{i}({c’})\times\omega_{k}^{c’}," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">hc</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover></msubsup></mrow><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∀</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>’</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>𝐲</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>’</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>k</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>’</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\omega_{i}^{\texttt{{hc}}}=\omega_{k}^{\hat{\mathbf{y}_{i}}},~\omega_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}=\sum_{\forall{c’}\in\xi_{i}}\tilde{\mathbf{y}}_{i}({c’})\times\omega_{k}^{c’},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmremark1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where for </span><math id="Thmremark1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{hc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">hc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{hc}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="Thmremark1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{\mathbf{y}_{i}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><msub><mi>𝐲</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{\mathbf{y}_{i}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> denotes argmax-derived category from its pseudo-label; for </span><math id="Thmremark1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="Thmremark1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\omega_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>ω</mi><mi>i</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\omega_{i}^{\texttt{{lc}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is designed to the weighted sum of proxy weights, based on the categories </span><math id="Thmremark1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> from </span><math id="Thmremark1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="Thmremark1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2) Negative Proxies Set.</span><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> For an unlabeled sample </span><math id="Thmremark1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> from batch </span><math id="Thmremark1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, any other sample </span><math id="Thmremark1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="j" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>j</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">j</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> in </span><math id="Thmremark1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{B}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℬ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{B}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> (including </span><math id="Thmremark1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{x}_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{x}_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">/</span><math id="Thmremark1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{j}^{\texttt{{hc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">hc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{j}^{\texttt{{hc}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">/</span><math id="Thmremark1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{u}_{j}^{\texttt{{lc}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>j</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_monospace">lc</mtext></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{u}_{j}^{\texttt{{lc}}}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">) will be considered as one of the negative-proxy candidates as long as its category set </span><math id="Thmremark1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> does not overlap with </span><math id="Thmremark1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\xi_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi_{i}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="Thmremark1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, i.e.,</span></p>
<table id="S5.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S5.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{R}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.E7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\left\{z_{j}~\middle|~\left[z_{j}=f_{m}(\mathbf{x}_{j};\theta_{m})\vee z_{j}=\right.\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.330em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐱</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∨</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>=</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\left\{z_{j}~\middle|~\left[z_{j}=f_{m}(\mathbf{x}_{j};\theta_{m})\vee z_{j}=\right.\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S5.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S5.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad\left.\left.f_{m}(\mathbf{u}_{j};\theta_{m})\right]\land\xi_{j}\cap\xi_{i}=\varnothing\right\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐮</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>θ</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo>∧</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∩</mo><msub><mi>ξ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo>}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad\left.\left.f_{m}(\mathbf{u}_{j};\theta_{m})\right]\land\xi_{j}\cap\xi_{i}=\varnothing\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<figure id="S5.T1" class="ltx_table">
