<figure id="S6.F5" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.21078v1/fig_exp-10313.png" id="S6.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:651/172;" width="651" height="172" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S6.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S6.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a) Distribution of labeled and unlabeled data across clients under <math id="S6.F5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.1</annotation></semantics></math> taking CIFAR-10 as an example. (b) Convergence curves of ProxyFL and other baselines on CIFAR-100 with <math id="S6.F5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. (c) Distribution of global category proxies before-and after-tuning visualized in a t-SNE plot.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.2 </span>Performance Comparison</h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tab. <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2.2 Positive-Negative Proxy Pool ‣ 5.2 Indecisive-Categories Proxy Learning ‣ 5 Methodology ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports the overall results of our ProxyFL and other state-of-the-art methods across multiple datasets under different Non-IID (<span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Non</span>-<span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>ndependent and <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I</span>dentical <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">D</span>istribution) scenarios with <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> label. We compare the following methods in our experiments like SAGE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>: <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">I. FL methods.</span> For FedAvg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>]</cite> and FedProx <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, all clients are trained via supervised federated learning only on partial labeled data; For FedAvg-SL, local data are all fully-labeled data, which denotes the ideal upper-bound based on standard fully-supervised FedAvg. <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">II. Vanilla combinations (FL + SSL methods).</span> Here, each method denotes a simple combination of SSL methods and FL methods. Note that FixMatch-LPL and FixMatch-GPL are both FixMatch-based frameworks, but pseudo-labels (PL) are derived from different models, <em id="S6.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">i.e.</em>, local model for LPL and global model for GPL, respectively. <span id="S6.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">III. FSSL methods.</span> We compare ProxyFL with previous state-of-the-art FSSL methods, including FedMatch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, FedLabel <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>]</cite>, FedLoke <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>, FedDure <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>]</cite>, FedDB <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and SAGE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In Tab. <a href="#S5.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 5.2.2 Positive-Negative Proxy Pool ‣ 5.2 Indecisive-Categories Proxy Learning ‣ 5 Methodology ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, ProxyFL achieves state-of-the-art performances on multiple datasets with significant improvements under different levels of data heterogeneity <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>. <span id="S6.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">To the best of our knowledge, we are the first in FSSL to propose category proxy for mitigating both internal and external heterogeneity.</span> Notably, our ProxyFL even achieves comparable performance to that of FedAvg-SL on SVHN dataset and CINIC-10 when <math id="S6.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. We attribute this improvement to the generalization of the enhanced category distribution from our proxy-guided FSSL framework.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">6.3 </span>Empirical Analysis</h3>

<div id="S6.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we conduct an in-depth investigation to assess the contributions of our GPT and ICPL in ProxyFL.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1) Effectiveness of modules</span> We first assess the contributions of GPT and ICPL through ablation experiments and set FedAvg+FixMatch-GPL as our baseline model. We conduct ablation studies under different levels of heterogeneity <math id="S6.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=\{0.1,~0.5,~1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo rspace="0.497em">,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=\{0.1,~0.5,~1\}</annotation></semantics></math> to validate the effectiveness of each module. As shown in Tab. <a href="#S6.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 6.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, each module could individually enhance model performance and their combination of GPT &amp; ICPL achieves the best results</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2) Convergence analysis</span> Fig. <a href="#S6.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 6.1 Experimental Setup ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>(b) and Tab. <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Analysis ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> demonstrate that ProxyFL substantially improves the convergence speed and test accuracy on the CIFAR-100 dataset with <math id="S6.SS3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=0.1</annotation></semantics></math>. ProxyFL outperforms baseline and current FSSL methods, achieving better performance with greater communication efficiency. Most existing FSSL methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> only retain high-confidence samples for training while discarding the low-confidence ones, leading to slower model convergence due to fewer training samples under non-IID scenarios. In contrast, ProxyFL cautiously incorporates low-confidence unlabeled samples into training and leverages category proxies to address both internal and external heterogeneity, accelerating model convergence especially during the early training stages (Tab. <a href="#S6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 6.3 Empirical Analysis ‣ 6 Experiments ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>). We also provide some <span id="S6.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">theoretical analysis &amp; proofs</span> in Appendix. <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Theoretical Proofs ‣ ProxyFL: A Proxy-Guided Framework for Federated Semi-Supervised Learning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
